<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Chapter 5 exercises.

     One flat list, numbered in order. Which exercises get assigned
     together lives in the course schedule, not here.

     Exercises 1-5 go with Sections 5.1-5.4 of the book (pages 1-3 of
     this chapter: page1-mixed-strategies, page2-tennis-other-methods,
     page3-poker). Exercises for 5.5 onward can be appended below;
     numbering of the existing ones will not shift.

     Sources:
       1. Computing a Mixed Equilibrium   - previous HW 8, Exercise 1
                                            (old part (c) is now a remark)
       2. A Symmetric 3x3 Game            - previous HW 8, Exercise 2
                                            (new numbers; set up, then solve)
       3. Active and Inactive Strategies  - previous HW 8, Problem 1
       4. A Two-Stage Prisoner's Dilemma  - previous HW 8, Problem 2
                                            (strategies renamed C, D, T;
                                            moves are lowercase c, d)
       5. A Simple Poker Game, Revisited  - new; Section 5.3.1 method,
                                            finishes hw-simple-poker (Ch. 4)

     Confidence checks: Exercises 1(a), 2(a), and 4(a) have a
     <solution> so students can confirm before moving on. Divisional
     solutions are hidden dropdowns (see publication.ptx). -->

<exercises xml:id="ch5-exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Many of these exercises use the Fundamental Theorem of Nash
      Equilibria (Theorem 5.2). As a reminder, a mixed strategy profile
      <m>\sigma</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly when, for every player
      <m>i</m>:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          (the <term>equality condition</term>) If <m>s_i</m> and
          <m>s_i'</m> are both active, then
          <m>\pi_i(s_i, \sigma_{-i}) = \pi_i(s_i', \sigma_{-i})</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          (the <term>inequality condition</term>) If <m>s_i</m> is active
          and <m>s_i'</m> is not, then
          <m>\pi_i(s_i, \sigma_{-i}) \geq \pi_i(s_i', \sigma_{-i})</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      A few parts have a <term>Solution</term> dropdown with the final
      answer. Use it to check your answer before moving on, since the
      later parts build on it. But make sure to try the part on your own
      first!
    </p>
    <p>
      Throughout, Player 1 is the row player and Player 2 is the column
      player, and payoffs are listed in the order (Player 1, Player 2).
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-compute-msne">
    <title>Computing a Mixed Equilibrium</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following game.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-compute-msne">
        <title>A <m>2 \times 2</m> game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>(2, 4)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>(1, 6)</cell>
            <cell>(3, 7)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-compute-msne-find">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the Fundamental Theorem of Nash Equilibria to find a mixed
          strategy Nash equilibrium in which both players use both of
          their strategies. Write Player 1's mixed strategy as
          <m>\sigma_1 = pa + (1-p)b</m> and Player 2's as
          <m>\sigma_2 = qx + (1-q)y</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\left(\frac{1}{5}a + \frac{4}{5}b,\ \frac{3}{4}x + \frac{1}{4}y\right)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-compute-msne-payoff">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is each player's expected payoff at the mixed strategy Nash
          equilibrium you found in part (a)?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          By Theorem 5.3, a player's equilibrium payoff equals what
          <em>any one</em> of their active strategies earns against the
          other player's mixed strategy. So you can compute, for example,
          <m>\pi_1(a, \sigma_2)</m> instead of adding up all four cells.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> This game also has two pure strategy Nash
        equilibria: <m>(a, x)</m>, with payoffs <m>(2, 4)</m>, and
        <m>(b, y)</m>, with payoffs <m>(3, 7)</m>. (You can check these
        with best response.) Comparing these with your answer to part (b),
        we see that both players prefer <m>(b, y)</m> to the mixed
        strategy equilibrium. So a game can have pure and mixed strategy Nash
        equilibria at the same time, and the mixed one isn't necessarily
        better for anyone.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-symmetric-3x3">
    <title>A Symmetric <m>3 \times 3</m> Game</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following two-player game.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-symmetric-3x3">
        <title>A symmetric <m>3 \times 3</m> game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>c</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>(2, 2)</cell>
            <cell>(6, 3)</cell>
            <cell>(5, 4)</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>(3, 6)</cell>
            <cell>(3, 3)</cell>
            <cell>(9, 4)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell>(4, 5)</cell>
            <cell>(4, 9)</cell>
            <cell>(7, 7)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        This game is <term>symmetric</term>: if the players swap
        strategies, they also swap payoffs. So you only need to do the
        computation for one player, and you get a <term>symmetric Nash
        equilibrium</term> <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>, where both players use
        the same mixed strategy <m>\sigma = xa + yb + zc</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-symmetric-3x3-system">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the Fundamental Theorem of Nash Equilibria to set up a system
          of linear equations whose solution gives a symmetric Nash
          equilibrium <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> in which all three
          strategies are active. Explain where each equation comes from.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <md>
          <mrow>2x + 6y + 5z = 3x + 3y + 9z \amp = 4x + 4y + 7z</mrow>
          <mrow>x + y + z \amp = 1</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-symmetric-3x3-solve">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve your system and state the Nash equilibrium
          <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        A symmetric game can also have Nash equilibria that are
        <em>not</em> symmetric. You have already seen this: Hawk-Dove
        (<xref ref="hw-hawk-dove-nash" />) is symmetric, and its Nash
        equilibria <m>(h, d)</m> and <m>(d, h)</m> are not symmetric.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-active-inactive">
    <title>Active and Inactive Strategies</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the game below, where Player 1 has three strategies and
        Player 2 has two.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-active-inactive">
        <title>A <m>3 \times 2</m> game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>(2, 5)</cell>
            <cell>(6, 4)</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>(5, 5)</cell>
            <cell>(3, 8)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell>(3, 7)</cell>
            <cell>(4, 9)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Throughout, write Player 2's mixed strategy as
        <m>\sigma_2 = qx + (1-q)y</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-active-inactive-three">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the equality condition of the Fundamental Theorem to show that
          there is no mixed strategy Nash equilibrium in which all three of
          Player 1's strategies are active.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-active-inactive-ab">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m>
          in which <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are active in <m>\sigma_1</m>,
          <m>c</m> is not active in <m>\sigma_1</m>, and <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m> are both active in <m>\sigma_2</m>. Write
          <m>\sigma_1 = pa + (1-p)b</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          You must check whether both conditions of Theorem 5.2 can be
          satisfied:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The equality condition on <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> determines
              the value of <m>q</m>. The equality condition on <m>x</m> and
              <m>y</m> determines the value of <m>p</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Check the inequality condition for every inactive strategy.
              Notice that <m>c</m> (for Player 1) is the only inactive
              strategy.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-active-inactive-ac">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that there is no mixed strategy Nash equilibrium in which
          <m>a</m> and <m>c</m> are active in <m>\sigma_1</m> and <m>b</m>
          is inactive.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          This is similar to part (b), with <m>a</m> and <m>c</m> active
          instead of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>. Use the equality condition for
          Player 1 to find the only possible <m>\sigma_2</m>. This time, the
          inequality condition should fail for <m>b</m>. (You don't need to
          find <m>\sigma_1</m>.)
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> In fact, the Nash equilibrium you found in
        part (b) is the <em>only</em> Nash equilibrium of this game. The
        remaining cases (<m>b</m> and <m>c</m> active, pure strategy
        profiles, or one player mixing while the other uses a pure
        strategy) can all be ruled out in the same way.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd">
    <title>A Two-Stage Prisoner's Dilemma</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following Prisoner's Dilemma, where each player can
        cooperate (<m>c</m>) or defect (<m>d</m>).
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-two-stage-pd-stage">
        <title>One stage of the game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>d</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell>(3, 3)</cell>
            <cell>(-1, 5)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>d</m></cell>
            <cell>(5, -1)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Now the players play this Prisoner's Dilemma <em>twice</em>, and
        each player's total payoff is the sum of their payoffs from the
        two stages. Both players see what happened in the first stage
        before the second stage starts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Because the game has two stages, <m>c</m> and <m>d</m> are now
        <em>moves</em> that a player makes in each stage. A
        <em>strategy</em> for the two-stage game has to say what move to
        make in each stage, and the move in the second stage can depend
        on what happened in the first. We will only consider three
        strategies (there are more). To keep them separate from the
        moves, we write strategies with capital letters:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>C</m>, always cooperate: play <m>c</m> in both stages.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>D</m>, always defect: play <m>d</m> in both stages.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>T</m>, tit for tat: play <m>c</m> in stage 1. In stage 2,
            copy the move the other player made in stage 1.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Restricting the players to these three strategies gives the
        following game in normal form, with two entries left blank.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-two-stage-pd">
        <title>The two-stage game, to be completed</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" width="15%" />
          <col right="minor" width="15%" />
          <col width="15%" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>C</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>D</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>T</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>C</m></cell>
            <cell>(6, 6)</cell>
            <cell>(-2, 10)</cell>
            <cell>(6, 6)</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>D</m></cell>
            <cell>(10, -2)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>T</m></cell>
            <cell>(6, 6)</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>(6, 6)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd-fill">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the payoffs for the strategy profiles <m>(D, T)</m> and
          <m>(T, D)</m>, and fill them in to complete the payoff matrix.
          Briefly explain what happens in each stage.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(D, T)</m> gives <m>(5, -1)</m> and <m>(T, D)</m> gives
          <m>(-1, 5)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd-pure">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use best response to find all pure strategy Nash equilibria.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd-equality">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now look for a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium
          <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m> in which, for each player, <m>C</m>
          and <m>T</m> are active and <m>D</m> is inactive:
          <me>
            \sigma_1 = pC + (1-p)T, \qquad \sigma_2 = qC + (1-q)T,
            \qquad 0 \lt p \lt 1,\ 0 \lt q \lt 1.
          </me>
          Show that the equality condition of the Fundamental Theorem holds
          for <em>every</em> <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> in <m>(0, 1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd-inequality">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the inequality condition to find exactly which values of
          <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> make <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m> a mixed
          strategy Nash equilibrium. Then explain in words what your answer
          says: how often can a player use always cooperate (<m>C</m>)
          before the other player would rather switch to always defect
          (<m>D</m>)?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> Like <xref ref="hw-symmetric-3x3" />, this
        is a symmetric game. But here there are infinitely many mixed
        strategy Nash equilibria, and most of them are not symmetric: if
        <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> both satisfy your answer to part (d) but
        <m>p \neq q</m>, the players are using different mixed strategies.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed">
    <title>A Simple Poker Game, Revisited</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="hw-simple-poker" /> we found that A Simple Poker
        Game has no Bayesian Nash equilibrium in pure strategies, and we
        promised that it would have one once we allowed mixed strategies.
        Let's find it, this time using the partial derivative method of
        Section 5.3.1 (<xref ref="read-5-3" text="title" />).
      </p>

      <p>
        Recall that Player 1's strategies say what he does with the high
        card and then with the low card. Since <m>FB</m> and <m>FF</m> are
        strictly dominated, we drop them, which leaves the following
        game:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-simple-poker-mixed">
        <title>A Simple Poker Game, with <m>FB</m> and <m>FF</m> removed</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>B</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>F</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>BB</m></cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
            <cell>(2, -2)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>BF</m></cell>
            <cell>(1, -1)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Write Player 1's mixed strategy as
        <m>\sigma_1 = p\,BB + (1-p)\,BF</m> and Player 2's as
        <m>\sigma_2 = qB + (1-q)F</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed-functions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write each player's expected payoff as a function of <m>p</m>
          and <m>q</m>: find <m>\pi_1(p, q)</m> and <m>\pi_2(p, q)</m>, as
          the book does for Tennis in Section 5.3.1.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed-derivatives">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the partial derivative method of Section 5.3.1 to find a
          mixed strategy Nash equilibrium with <m>0 \lt p \lt 1</m> and
          <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m>. Which partial derivatives do you set equal
          to <m>0</m>, and why those ones?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed-payoff">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find Player 1's expected payoff at the Nash equilibrium. Then,
          without doing a second computation, find Player 2's expected
          payoff, and explain why you only needed to compute one of them.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          In every cell of the table, the two payoffs add to <m>0</m>. So
          for every strategy profile, including mixed ones, Player 2's
          payoff is the negative of Player 1's:
          <m>\pi_2 = -\pi_1</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed-interpret">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Interpret the equilibrium:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              What does Player 1 do when he has the high card?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              When he has the low card, how often does he bid anyway (that
              is, bluff)?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              How often does Player 2 bid back?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> Player 2 can never come out ahead in this
        game: none of Player 2's payoffs in the table are positive. The
        best Player 2 can do is keep the losses small, and the equilibrium
        mix does exactly that.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-ex" iframe="question-form.html?section=chapter 5 exercises" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</exercises>
